window.docContents[13].resolve({"/Imp/#Hoare-Logic--Imp___-Simple-Imperative-Programs--Expressions-With-Variables--Syntax":{"contents":"We can add variables to the arithmetic expressions we had before simply\nby including one more constructor.  (This is a fresh Aexp, replacing\nthe variable-free one from the Slang chapter.)\n\n\n\ninductive Aexp where\n  | num (n : Nat)\n  | id (x : Ident)                -- NEW\n  | plus (a1 a2 : Aexp)\n  | minus (a1 a2 : Aexp)\n  | mult (a1 a2 : Aexp)\n\n\n\n\nThe Bexp definition is unchanged, except that it now refers to the new Aexp.\n\ninductive Bexp where\n  | bool (b : Bool)\n  | eq (a1 a2 : Aexp)\n  | neq (a1 a2 : Aexp)\n  | le (a1 a2 : Aexp)\n  | gt (a1 a2 : Aexp)\n  | not (b : Bexp)\n  | and (b1 b2 : Bexp)\n\n\nDefining a few variable names as shorthands will make examples easier to read.\n\ndef W : Ident := \"W\"\ndef X : Ident := \"X\"\ndef Y : Ident := \"Y\"\ndef Z : Ident := \"Z\"\n\n\n(This convention for naming program variables (X, Y, Z) clashes a\nbit with our earlier use of uppercase letters for types. Since we're not\nusing polymorphism heavily in the chapters developed to Imp, this\noverloading should not cause confusion.)\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Imp: Simple Imperative Programs\u0009Expressions With Variables","header":"3.1.2. Syntax","id":"/Imp/#Hoare-Logic--Imp___-Simple-Imperative-Programs--Expressions-With-Variables--Syntax"}});